---
source: crates/deino-export/src/subtitles.rs
expression: srt(&export)
---
1
00:00:01,507 --> 00:00:05,102
Shreyas: Hey Maya. How are you doing? Like, honestly.

2
00:00:05,594 --> 00:00:11,774
Maya Chen: Honestly? Pretty wiped. I slept eleven hours on Monday night and still woke up tired.

3
00:00:12,547 --> 00:00:18,302
Shreyas: Yeah, I'm not surprised. You carried most of that Fernhollow incident on your own.

4
00:00:19,187 --> 00:00:27,607
Maya Chen: It was a lot of three a.m. pages. And every time I thought it was fixed, another batch of duplicates would show up.

5
00:00:28,349 --> 00:00:34,574
Shreyas: So I want to start there, before we get into Sync v2 or anything else. Workload first.

6
00:00:35,382 --> 00:00:37,297
Maya Chen: Okay. Yeah, that's fair.

7
00:00:37,869 --> 00:00:46,399
Shreyas: My proposal is you come off the on-call rotation for the next two weeks. Starting today, and you're back on the twenty-first.

8
00:00:46,984 --> 00:00:53,929
Maya Chen: Wait, really? Who picks up my shifts, though? Priya's already got SOC 2 and the release cuts.

9
00:00:54,587 --> 00:01:03,587
Shreyas: I'll take two of them myself, and Diego said he's happy to take the weekend. I asked him yesterday before I said anything to you.

10
00:01:04,377 --> 00:01:11,857
Maya Chen: Diego on the backend pager feels a little scary. No offense to Diego, he's great, it's just not his code.

11
00:01:12,614 --> 00:01:23,409
Shreyas: Fair. He'd be secondary, with me as primary for anything sync related. And the runbook you wrote last week is genuinely good, so I think he'll be fine.

12
00:01:24,167 --> 00:01:30,427
Maya Chen: Okay. Okay, then yes. Thank you. I think I need it more than I want to admit.

13
00:01:31,042 --> 00:01:40,842
Shreyas: Then it's decided. You're off the rotation for two weeks. I'll update the schedule in PagerDuty this afternoon and post it in the eng channel.

14
00:01:41,232 --> 00:01:48,112
Maya Chen: Can I still look at the alerts if something sync related goes sideways? I'll feel weird not looking.

15
00:01:48,604 --> 00:01:57,169
Shreyas: Look, sure. Get paged, no. If something is actually on fire I'll call you, but that's my call to make, not yours.

16
00:01:58,066 --> 00:01:59,771
Maya Chen: Yeah, that's fair enough.

17
00:02:00,472 --> 00:02:05,797
Shreyas: And take Friday off. Seriously. You worked basically all of last weekend.

18
00:02:06,319 --> 00:02:15,749
Maya Chen: I was going to say no, but, um, actually, yes. I'll take Friday off. My sister's been asking me to come up for like a month.

19
00:02:16,402 --> 00:02:23,962
Shreyas: Perfect, go. Anything else on workload? Like, is there stuff on your plate right now that shouldn't be?

20
00:02:24,564 --> 00:02:36,079
Maya Chen: The interview loop, a bit. I've done four phone screens in the last two weeks on top of everything else, and each one is like an hour and a half with the write-up.

21
00:02:36,479 --> 00:02:44,949
Shreyas: Let's pause you on screens until the end of the month. I'll tell the recruiter today to take you off the screening rotation.

22
00:02:45,475 --> 00:02:54,870
Maya Chen: I'd still like to do the final rounds for the senior backend role, though. That person's going to be working with me on sync every day.

23
00:02:55,355 --> 00:02:58,565
Shreyas: Makes total sense. Finals only, no screens.

24
00:02:59,256 --> 00:03:01,141
Maya Chen: Great, thank you, really.

25
00:03:01,514 --> 00:03:11,349
Shreyas: Okay. How are you feeling about the incident itself? Not the hours, the, like, the bug. I know it's been a weird couple of weeks.

26
00:03:11,889 --> 00:03:22,789
Maya Chen: Mixed. I shipped the change that caused it, so there's a part of me that's just embarrassed. A hundred and thirty duplicate jobs at one customer is a lot.

27
00:03:23,134 --> 00:03:34,679
Maya Chen: But I also think the fix is solid. Deduping on the iCalUID plus the recurrence ID is what we should have done from day one, and the cleanup script worked first time.

28
00:03:35,271 --> 00:03:46,911
Shreyas: For what it's worth, I don't see it as your bug. Two people reviewed that PR, including me, and none of us had a test with a moved occurrence of a recurring event.

29
00:03:47,209 --> 00:03:53,159
Shreyas: That's a process gap. It'll be in the postmortem as a process gap, not as a person.

30
00:03:53,972 --> 00:03:59,762
Maya Chen: Thanks. That helps, actually. Is the postmortem still going to Owen on Friday?

31
00:04:00,148 --> 00:04:07,668
Shreyas: Yeah, I'm writing it. I'll send you the draft tomorrow so you can check I haven't mangled the technical bits.

32
00:04:08,306 --> 00:04:12,406
Maya Chen: Sure, but I'm off Friday, so I'll read it tomorrow evening.

33
00:04:13,097 --> 00:04:17,942
Shreyas: Tomorrow evening is fine. Don't rewrite it, just flag anything wrong.

34
00:04:18,128 --> 00:04:25,813
Shreyas: Before we move on, is there anything I could have done better during the incident? I want the honest version.

35
00:04:26,344 --> 00:04:39,099
Maya Chen: Honestly, yes. The worst part wasn't the pages, it was everyone pinging me directly. Tom, Lena, Aisha, you. Sometimes four people asking the same question within ten minutes.

36
00:04:39,306 --> 00:04:46,171
Maya Chen: I'd be in the middle of reading logs and I'd have to stop and write the same status update four times.

37
00:04:46,714 --> 00:04:51,959
Shreyas: Yeah. That's on me. I should have set up a single channel on day one.

38
00:04:52,189 --> 00:05:03,299
Shreyas: So going forward, for any customer-impacting incident, Tom owns customer updates, I own internal updates, and whoever is fixing it only talks in the incident channel.

39
00:05:04,115 --> 00:05:14,725
Maya Chen: That would've saved me hours. Literally hours. And maybe a fixed cadence, like hourly, so people know when the next update is coming and stop asking.

40
00:05:15,267 --> 00:05:25,357
Shreyas: Yes, hourly updates while it's active. Good idea. I'll write all of that into the incident runbook before you're back on call on the twenty-first.

41
00:05:25,508 --> 00:05:30,693
Shreyas: Okay. Calendar Sync v2. Where are we, and are the dates still real?

42
00:05:31,468 --> 00:05:38,718
Maya Chen: The dates are still real, I think. Beta on the thirteenth of November, GA on the fourth of December.

43
00:05:39,088 --> 00:05:51,118
Maya Chen: The Google side is in decent shape. Push notifications are working in my branch, and with the dedupe work from the incident we basically got the event identity piece for free.

44
00:05:51,506 --> 00:05:53,821
Shreyas: The iCalUID plus recurrence ID key.

45
00:05:54,374 --> 00:06:03,059
Maya Chen: Exactly. That's now the canonical key for every event we ingest, from both providers. So that part of the design is done.

46
00:06:03,333 --> 00:06:11,413
Maya Chen: Google push channels expire too, but they last about a week and the renewal is boring. I'm not worried about Google.

47
00:06:11,858 --> 00:06:17,728
Shreyas: How are you thinking about the cutover from polling? I really don't want a big bang.

48
00:06:18,169 --> 00:06:27,089
Maya Chen: No, definitely not. For beta customers we keep the five-minute polling running alongside push, and we compare what each one sees.

49
00:06:27,253 --> 00:06:39,208
Maya Chen: If push and polling disagree, we log it and trust polling. Once the disagreement rate is basically zero for a week, we turn polling down to once an hour as a safety net.

50
00:06:39,611 --> 00:06:44,416
Shreyas: Love that. It also gives us an actual number to look at before GA.

51
00:06:45,221 --> 00:06:50,826
Maya Chen: Right. I want to show a chart of that disagreement rate at the GA go or no-go.

52
00:06:51,022 --> 00:07:02,427
Maya Chen: Oh, and a nice side effect. With polling every five minutes we're using about forty percent of our daily Google API quota. With push it should drop to under ten.

53
00:07:03,250 --> 00:07:09,980
Shreyas: That's a good number for the board deck, honestly. Write that one down somewhere I can find it.

54
00:07:10,517 --> 00:07:22,152
Maya Chen: And the recurring-event test harness I proposed, I'd like to build that into v2 from the start. Moved occurrences, cancelled occurrences, time zone changes on the series.

55
00:07:22,811 --> 00:07:28,836
Shreyas: Yes. Make it part of the v2 scope. That's exactly what would have caught this bug.

56
00:07:29,002 --> 00:07:30,637
Shreyas: And what about Microsoft?

57
00:07:31,467 --> 00:07:42,432
Maya Chen: Microsoft is where the risk is. Graph webhook subscriptions for calendar events expire after roughly three days. The max is actually a little under three days.

58
00:07:42,669 --> 00:07:53,589
Maya Chen: So every subscription, for every technician, has to be renewed before it lapses. And if we miss one, we just silently stop getting changes for that person.

59
00:07:53,972 --> 00:07:56,177
Shreyas: Silently is the scary word there.

60
00:07:56,773 --> 00:08:08,698
Maya Chen: Yeah. Fernhollow alone is sixty technicians, and if the Brightkite deal closes that's another two hundred and twenty. Across all customers that's thousands of renewals a week.

61
00:08:09,378 --> 00:08:11,058
Shreyas: Okay. What's the plan?

62
00:08:11,557 --> 00:08:20,077
Maya Chen: Three layers. First, a renewal job that runs every six hours and renews anything that expires in the next twenty-four hours.

63
00:08:20,391 --> 00:08:32,241
Maya Chen: Second, a handler for Microsoft's lifecycle notifications. They tell you when a subscription is about to be removed or needs reauthorizing, and we should just react to those.

64
00:08:32,421 --> 00:08:42,391
Maya Chen: And third, a reconciliation sweep once a day that does a delta query per calendar. So even if a subscription dies, we catch up within a day.

65
00:08:42,881 --> 00:08:51,886
Shreyas: A day feels long for a dispatcher, though. If a tech's afternoon changes and we don't see it until tomorrow, that's a missed job.

66
00:08:52,590 --> 00:09:02,970
Maya Chen: Hmm. Yeah, that's fair. I could run the sweep every hour for anyone whose subscription looks unhealthy, and keep the daily one for everybody else.

67
00:09:03,572 --> 00:09:11,052
Shreyas: I like that. And can we alert if more than, say, one percent of subscriptions are expired at any point?

68
00:09:11,484 --> 00:09:17,479
Maya Chen: Sure, that's easy. I'll put the hourly sweep and the one percent alert in the design.

69
00:09:18,265 --> 00:09:20,480
Shreyas: Is any of this written down yet?

70
00:09:21,072 --> 00:09:28,137
Maya Chen: About half. I'll finish the renewal section of the design doc by Monday and send it to you and Priya.

71
00:09:28,970 --> 00:09:35,585
Shreyas: Monday the twelfth, great. Priya should look at the webhook endpoint side anyway, for SOC 2.

72
00:09:35,952 --> 00:09:44,307
Maya Chen: Yeah, she'll want to know how we validate the client state on incoming notifications. I already have a paragraph on that.

73
00:09:44,654 --> 00:09:54,459
Maya Chen: One idea I had, um, was shipping the beta Google-only and adding Microsoft at GA. It would take a lot of pressure off the next five weeks.

74
00:09:55,309 --> 00:10:04,869
Shreyas: Hmm. I don't love it, because I think about a third of the beta customers are on Microsoft 365. But I don't want to decide it today.

75
00:10:05,175 --> 00:10:10,750
Shreyas: Let's look at it again in two weeks, once we know how the renewal work is going.

76
00:10:11,200 --> 00:10:12,520
Maya Chen: Okay, fine by me.

77
00:10:13,166 --> 00:10:25,871
Shreyas: Separate question. Long term, should there be a second person who really knows sync? Because right now it's basically you, and that's part of why the last three weeks were so rough.

78
00:10:26,350 --> 00:10:35,375
Maya Chen: Yes, definitely. Diego's said he'd like to do more backend. Maybe after offline mode ships he could rotate onto sync for a bit?

79
00:10:36,156 --> 00:10:45,176
Shreyas: Maybe. Let's see how offline mode goes first. I don't want to promise him something and then pull him back to mobile in January.

80
00:10:45,816 --> 00:10:48,581
Maya Chen: Fair. I'll not mention it to him yet.

81
00:10:48,994 --> 00:10:54,864
Shreyas: Did you ever look at any of the unified calendar API vendors? Just as a comparison.

82
00:10:55,602 --> 00:11:07,722
Maya Chen: Briefly. They charge per connected account, so at our size it'd be a few thousand dollars a month, and we'd be depending on someone else for the exact thing that just bit us.

83
00:11:08,462 --> 00:11:11,112
Shreyas: Yeah. Probably not worth it, then.

84
00:11:11,610 --> 00:11:16,940
Maya Chen: Probably not. Which, sort of, brings me to the thing I wanted to raise.

85
00:11:17,139 --> 00:11:26,054
Maya Chen: I think we should bring in a contractor for the Microsoft side. Six to eight weeks, someone who's done Graph subscriptions before.

86
00:11:26,651 --> 00:11:28,166
Shreyas: Okay. Tell me why.

87
00:11:28,875 --> 00:11:42,230
Maya Chen: Because right now it's me and half of Diego, and Diego's mostly on offline mode. If I get sick, or something else blows up like last month, the beta date slips. A contractor de-risks that.

88
00:11:42,601 --> 00:11:49,956
Shreyas: I get it, and I think the worry is right. I just don't think a contractor fixes it. A few reasons.

89
00:11:50,333 --> 00:11:59,208
Shreyas: One, ramp-up. By the time someone understands our sync model and the job matching, it's mid-November and the beta is already out.

90
00:11:59,618 --> 00:12:14,373
Shreyas: Two, the SOC 2 observation window starts on the first of November. Any contractor with production access is in scope, so Priya would be doing access reviews and background checks for them right in the middle of it.

91
00:12:15,137 --> 00:12:21,267
Maya Chen: They wouldn't need production access, though. They could build against the sandbox tenant.

92
00:12:21,822 --> 00:12:30,617
Shreyas: For building, maybe. But for debugging renewals in the real world you need prod. That's where all the weird stuff shows up.

93
00:12:31,363 --> 00:12:37,048
Maya Chen: Hmm. That's, yeah, that's true. Last week was nothing but prod-only weirdness.

94
00:12:37,752 --> 00:12:50,687
Shreyas: And three, money. A good contractor who knows Graph is fifteen to twenty thousand a month. That's a big chunk of a senior hire, and the board already approved the senior backend role.

95
00:12:51,542 --> 00:13:02,022
Maya Chen: But the senior hire won't be in seat until January at the earliest. The next eight weeks are the risky part, and a hire doesn't help with that at all.

96
00:13:02,576 --> 00:13:10,096
Shreyas: That's fair, and I'm not dismissing it. What if we handle the next eight weeks with scope instead of people?

97
00:13:10,382 --> 00:13:22,282
Shreyas: If renewal looks shaky at the two-week check, Microsoft slips to GA. That's the release valve. And in the meantime all the hiring energy goes into the senior backend role.

98
00:13:22,914 --> 00:13:26,604
Maya Chen: So the Google-only beta is the fallback, not the plan.

99
00:13:27,334 --> 00:13:30,094
Shreyas: Exactly. Not the plan, the fallback.

100
00:13:30,557 --> 00:13:38,842
Maya Chen: Can I push back one more time, though? Even a part-time contractor, like two days a week, just on the renewal job?

101
00:13:39,559 --> 00:13:52,794
Shreyas: I hear you. But the renewal job is exactly the piece that needs prod and needs to be owned long term. I'd rather that's you now and the new hire later, not someone who leaves in December.

102
00:13:53,289 --> 00:13:59,114
Maya Chen: Yeah. Okay, that's actually the argument that lands for me. Ownership. Fine.

103
00:13:59,276 --> 00:14:09,161
Maya Chen: Okay. I still think a contractor would've been nice, but I can live with that. No contractor, and we put everything into the senior hire.

104
00:14:09,596 --> 00:14:20,386
Shreyas: Thank you. I'll talk to Aisha tomorrow about making the senior backend role the priority over the mobile one, and getting the recruiter to push harder on it.

105
00:14:21,016 --> 00:14:25,031
Maya Chen: And I want to be in the finals. I'm serious about that.

106
00:14:25,623 --> 00:14:28,203
Shreyas: You will be. That's already decided.

107
00:14:28,710 --> 00:14:36,640
Maya Chen: Can I ask how the board meeting went, by the way? People have been speculating in Slack, which is never great.

108
00:14:37,203 --> 00:14:48,498
Shreyas: It went well, honestly. They approved both hires, the senior backend one and the mobile one, and we're not raising now. Series A conversations start in March.

109
00:14:48,726 --> 00:14:59,566
Shreyas: Runway's about nineteen months, so nobody is panicking. The Fernhollow thing came up, but more as a question about how we handle incidents than anything else.

110
00:15:00,357 --> 00:15:05,697
Maya Chen: Okay, good. That's reassuring. I was half expecting a speech about burn.

111
00:15:06,371 --> 00:15:11,191
Shreyas: There's always a bit of a speech about burn. But no, it was fine.

112
00:15:11,452 --> 00:15:15,587
Shreyas: Okay, last thing, and it's the fun one. Staff engineer.

113
00:15:16,254 --> 00:15:18,829
Maya Chen: Oh. Okay. Hi, that's exciting.

114
00:15:19,320 --> 00:15:33,235
Shreyas: I think you're already operating at staff level. Sync v2 is a cross-team project, you led the incident, you wrote the runbook, and you've been mentoring Diego on the backend without anyone asking you to.

115
00:15:33,604 --> 00:15:41,919
Shreyas: Diego actually told me last week that the API pairing sessions with you were the most useful thing he's done since he joined.

116
00:15:42,690 --> 00:15:48,670
Maya Chen: Oh, that's sweet. He's picking up Rust really fast, actually. Faster than I did.

117
00:15:49,022 --> 00:15:55,497
Maya Chen: That's really nice to hear. Honestly I've been wondering if the incident would count against me.

118
00:15:56,217 --> 00:16:10,227
Shreyas: The opposite. The bug shipped, sure, but the way you handled it is exactly the staff-level part. Clear updates, a real fix plus the cleanup, and you already proposed the recurring-event test harness.

119
00:16:10,747 --> 00:16:16,127
Maya Chen: So what does the process look like? We don't really have a process, do we?

120
00:16:16,729 --> 00:16:28,599
Shreyas: Not much of one at fourteen people. I write a promo doc, Aisha reviews it, and it becomes effective at the start of a quarter. So realistically the first of January.

121
00:16:28,983 --> 00:16:36,288
Shreyas: I'll have a draft of the promo doc to you by next Friday. Then you can tell me where I've undersold you.

122
00:16:37,156 --> 00:16:39,916
Maya Chen: Next Friday, so the sixteenth. Okay.

123
00:16:40,305 --> 00:16:47,885
Shreyas: It would help if you sent me a list of the things you're proudest of from this year. Bullet points are fine.

124
00:16:48,577 --> 00:16:54,757
Maya Chen: I can send that by Monday. I actually keep a brag doc, so it's mostly copy and paste.

125
00:16:55,545 --> 00:16:56,740
Shreyas: Of course you do.

126
00:16:57,598 --> 00:17:08,443
Maya Chen: Can I ask what staff actually looks like day to day here? Like, will I still be writing code? Because I don't want to become a full-time meetings person.

127
00:17:08,883 --> 00:17:21,373
Shreyas: Yes, you'd still be coding most of the time. At our size staff means you own the hardest technical area end to end and you make the people around you better. Not more meetings.

128
00:17:21,950 --> 00:17:24,300
Maya Chen: Okay. Good. That I can do.

129
00:17:24,492 --> 00:17:28,787
Maya Chen: Is there anything you think I'm missing? For staff, I mean.

130
00:17:29,172 --> 00:17:39,982
Shreyas: Honestly, writing for people outside engineering. Like, the postmortem I'm doing this week, I'd love for you to write the next one of those for a customer.

131
00:17:40,556 --> 00:17:46,826
Maya Chen: Yeah, that's fair. I tend to write for engineers and then wonder why sales has questions.

132
00:17:47,231 --> 00:17:55,026
Shreyas: And maybe present Sync v2 at the all-hands in November. Not committing you to it, just something to think about.

133
00:17:55,506 --> 00:17:59,896
Maya Chen: I'll think about it. Public speaking is not my favourite thing.

134
00:18:00,631 --> 00:18:06,551
Shreyas: No pressure. Okay, we're about at time. Let me recap so I don't forget anything.

135
00:18:06,708 --> 00:18:18,653
Shreyas: You're off on-call for two weeks starting today, back on the twenty-first. Friday off. No screens, finals only. No contractor, we push the senior backend hire instead.

136
00:18:18,976 --> 00:18:28,096
Shreyas: You send the renewal design and your brag list by Monday. I do the promo doc draft by next Friday, and I talk to Aisha tomorrow.

137
00:18:28,872 --> 00:18:30,902
Maya Chen: And you update PagerDuty today.

138
00:18:31,483 --> 00:18:34,813
Shreyas: And I update PagerDuty today. Go rest, Maya.

139
00:18:35,221 --> 00:18:38,066
Shreyas: Where does your sister live, by the way?

140
00:18:38,554 --> 00:18:46,884
Maya Chen: Just outside Portland. She's got a new puppy, so the plan is basically to sit on the floor with a dog for three days.

141
00:18:47,479 --> 00:18:50,574
Shreyas: That sounds like exactly the right prescription.

142
00:18:51,284 --> 00:18:55,374
Maya Chen: Thanks, Shreyas. Really. See you Thursday at standup.
